Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  abandon all hope ye who enter here  this is the warning inscribed above the  gates of hell it's from dante's inferno  an epic description of the medieval  underworld whilst dante envisioned it as  a large looming door several  illustrations depicted the entrance to  hell as a giant mouth a gaping set of  jaws that devours the souls of the  damned these helmets are everywhere  cropping up in manuscripts prayer books  and paintings  even in early theater productions where  giant mechanical mouths were constructed  in medieval town squares  but where did this fighting image come  from many believe that the hellmouth has  its roots in norse mythology with its  hellish ideas of man-eating wolves and  devouring dragons  welcome to strange christian art this  episode hellmouths  [Music]  open up a medieval manuscript and you're  never too far away from spotting a hell  mouth or two there are some with two  heads some with cauldrons inside and  some who've bitten off more than they  can chew but my favorite is the one with  a mouth in another mouth in a castle in  another mouth  many of our helmets are from illustrated  prayer books particularly in sections  about prayers for dead people often  you'll find these uniquely disturbing  representations of hell  these lavishly decorated books would  have taken months if not years to  complete and were typically owned by  very wealthy patrons for those who  couldn't afford such things you could  get your hellmouth fix at the theatre  there are accounts of medieval stage  productions featuring these giant  mechanical mouths some could swallow  actors others were able to billow smoke  and shoot fireworks these so-called  mystery plays were designed to amaze and  frighten ordinary townsfolk using early  special effects to deter an illiterate  population from bad behavior you might  have guessed this already but the core  purpose of these helmets is to scare  people to frighten them into faith  enough so that they think twice about  skipping church on sunday the idea of  being eaten by gigantic monster mouth is  indeed a horrifying possibility  then again medieval artists also  pictured heaven as the domain of giant  eagles made out of human faces  i'll take my chances with the helmets  another place where helmets appear are  in the many illustrations of jesus  leading people out of its jaws this  popular image is known as the harrowing  of hell  it's based on the christian myth that  between his crucifixion and resurrection  jesus took a trip to hell to rescue  those who were born before his ministry  there are loads of images like this  where these monstrous mouths seem to be  spewing out naked people  these gruesome mouths reveal how  medieval christians understood hell  many artworks envision hell as one giant  kitchen  victims are thrown into a giant cauldron  cooked on a flaming grill and finally  devoured by satan and his demons mostly  though it's satan doing all the eating  in artworks you can see him lurking at  the bottom happily chomping down on  human remains  it's hell's kitchen but literal who'da  thought the gordon ramsay show is so  rooted in art history the idea that hell  is one macabre banquet was taken up in  the gruesome paintings of hieronymus  bosch as well as peter brugel the elder  their disturbing renaissance hellscapes  are full of demons gorging themselves on  unfortunate humans  and if you look carefully you'll even  spot the odd hellmouth  [Music]  the thing that's unclear however is why  depict hell as a giant mouth it's  undoubtedly a disturbing image but where  did it come from some of the earliest  depictions of hellmouths are from saxon  england although they might have been  inherited from norse mythology you see  in saxon times large swathes of england  were ruled by viking raiders  particularly in the north in a territory  known as the danelaw  it's why so many english words have  norse roots ransack berserk slaughter  and heathen are all scandinavian in  origin  even the word hell itself comes from old  norse it's the name of the goddess of  death who rules the underworld realm of  the same name  but it's more than just the word itself  that we got from the vikings even some  religious ideas about hell have roots in  norse mythology  in a small unassuming graveyard in the  north of england there is a stone  monument called the gosforth cross it's  particularly ancient having been built  by saxon christians in the 900s  despite it being a christian monument it  also depicts scenes from norse mythology  like the death of balder at the hands of  the trickster god loki who is later  imprisoned for his crimes  and then there's this one a scene where  the warrior god vithar tears open the  jaws of the great wolf fenrir  it is remarkably similar to those  christian depictions of the harrowing of  hell where christ similarly rends open  the jaws of a hellmouth  this jewel between vithar and the wolf  fenrir could be a precursor to the  christian hellmouth it might explain why  so many these hellmouths are so canine  in appearance they could have originally  been borrowed from norse iconography and  the great wolf fenrir the gosforth cross  could have been used to convert the  local viking population to christianity  and point out some similarities between  the two religions like the death of  baldur and the crucifixion of jesus or  the binding of loki and the imprisonment  of satan in the book of revelation  the gospel cross is one example of the  cultural effect the vikings had on saxon  christianity  other norse ideas about the afterlife  seem to have made their way in as well  in an anglo-saxon chronicle the devil is  likened to a dragon that devours wicked  people  this description is rather similar to  the norse creature nidhogg a great  dragon who lives at the bottom of the  world in a realm called nastrond the  corpse shaw trapped in this gloomy  cavern nidhogg chews on the corpses of  the wicked a grisly fate reserved for  the worst criminals in viking society  as with before it's believed that this  norse idea about the underworld might  have diffused into saxon christianity  and it might explain why those saxon  chronicles had such a similar  understanding of hell as the vikings  from giant canine mouths to greedy  corpse gobbling dragons these  christianized ideas spread from england  onto the  continent whilst our christian hellmouth  has its roots in norse iconography the  idea of a devouring monster and the  underworld isn't wholly unique to  scandinavia in the egyptian book of the  dead the hearts of the unworthy are fed  to the demon s amit her body is a mix  between a hippo a lion and a crocodile  the most ferocious beasts of the nile  the archetype of the devouring god is  present in various world mythologies in  some cases they seem to have arisen  independently of one another  all express the same idea that being  eaten is equated with spiritual death  that's what makes the christian  hellmouth such a powerful image and a  useful deterrent against wrongdoing  it appeals to a very basic human  instinct don't get eaten  in this life or the next  thanks for watching i had great fun  making this and i hope you enjoyed it  too  if you like this video and want to see  more why not subscribe a like and a  comment also go a really long way  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time goodbye

